## Part B: Points referees will very likely raise

### B1. Outcome definition for football
Championship matches produce many draws. Define "binary match outcome": home win vs not, or draws excluded? This affects class balance and therefore accuracy-based ΔML.

### B2. Uncertainty on ΔML
- There are roughly 288 URC games (16 teams × 18 rounds ÷ 2 × 2 seasons) and about 1,100 Championship games.
- The binomial SE of accuracy is therefore several percentage points in rugby.
- Exemplars need confidence intervals or a paired test (McNemar, or a fold-level paired comparison). Otherwise "near-zero" (Q4) and the Q1–Q3 signs cannot be distinguished from noise.
- Report a proper scoring rule (log-loss/Brier) and AUC alongside accuracy, plus the majority-class baseline.

### B3. Exemplar selection
- Hand-picked exemplars will be read as cherry-picked unless the selection rule was pre-specified and stated.
- The Q4 exemplar sits on the κ = 1 boundary, so there is no clear empirical Q4 demonstration.
- The SI bootstrap uses **tackles** as the Q4 case. Why not make tackles the exemplar?
- The Q2 and Q4 exemplars (long balls, goalkeeper long balls) are overlapping constructs.

### B4. The absolute comparator
Comparing X_A − X_B against X_A alone is unusual. The natural absolute model uses (X_A, X_B), which nests the difference model under the constraint β_A = −β_B. This is flagged as out of scope, but expect a referee to request it. It is the comparison practitioners make (cf. Brito's "isolated team statistics").

### B5. Team-blocked CV is only half-blocked
- Each team's away matches remain in training when its home matches are held out, so team identity still leaks.
- For a one-feature, two-parameter logistic model, leakage likely matters little anyway. Describe it more modestly than "removes the dominant source of dependence".
- The cluster bootstrap has the same home-only structure.
- B = 300 is small; 1,000–2,000 is conventional.

### B6. Normality evidence
- Mean Shapiro–Wilk W is a weak summary at n ≈ 1,100.
- Report the proportion of KPIs rejecting normality, or QQ-plot summaries.
- Low-count KPIs (e.g. goalkeeper long balls) are the most likely to be visibly discrete.

### B7. Genomics selection bias
Ranking genes by paired-differential signal preferentially selects small Var(difference), i.e. high ρ, which inflates η. State this, or select genes by a ρ-independent criterion.

### B8. Novelty framing
The provenance is acknowledged (variance of a difference, Fisher, Pitman–Morgan, Pitman ARE of paired vs two-sample tests), but a referee may still call the formula textbook. The genuinely new contributions are the feature-construction reframing and the (d_rel, r) surface. Leaning into the latter (A6) addresses this directly.

